  "query_preview": "..\n\nSoutheast Michigan remains positioned along the active periphery of \na broad ridge currently centered over the southern Plains. Modest \nnorthwest flow aloft lingers as several shortwave perturbations work \nthrough the mean flow later today. The resident airmass remains \nmoist and conditionally unstable, characterized by surface dewpoints \nin the upper 60s while a stalled frontal boundary lingers over Lower \nMichigan. With dewpoint depressions near zero and little to know \ngradient flow, areas of locally dense fog should continue for \nseveral more hours early this morning. Opted for a last minute Dense \nFog Advisory along/south of I-69.\n\nForecast confidence remains low regarding convective evolution over \nthe next 12 hours, in line with the last few days. Latest CAM \nsolutions imply preference toward a persistence forecast approach, \nfocusing on areas south of I-94 as a High Plains MCS should survive \ninto the Ohio Valley. This runs contrary to SPC's Marginal Risk \nhighlighting locations north of I-94. However, scattered activity \ncannot be ruled out for the northern portion of the CWA once the \naforementioned front drifts southward as a weak cold front. The \nforcing along the frontal slope could trigger some attendant pop-up \nshowers/thunderstorms within an area of stronger shear, steeper mid-\nlevel lapse rates, and higher instability. A water-loaded core or \noverachieving updraft may suffice for a borderline severe wind gust \nor hailstone. AI/ML models differ significantly, emphasizing the \nuncertainty between both areas of focus. Coverage should wane late \nthis evening or early tonight.\n\nThe existing synoptic ridge axis begins to roll eastward Thursday, \nbut will be reinforced by amplification over western CONUS. Mainly \ndry conditions are forecast for Thursday with high pressure building \nin, albeit the Ohio border area could still be susceptible to \nglancing convection from the perpetual MCS train tracks rolling \nthrough Indiana and Ohio. Low predictability exists to close out the \nweek and over much of the weekend as flow weakens aloft and the \nlongwave ridge gets flattened by a Canadian wave. The best chance \nfor another moisture/warm advection surge arrives late Saturday into \nearly Sunday as the Canadian trough tracks into Ontario with a cold \nfront extending into the Great Lakes. Potential exists for organized \nconvection with the frontal passage. A wind shift from south to \nnorthwest and a drop in temperatures and dewpoints to follow by \nearly next week.",
